The current position has 12 pieces on the board (6 for each player), with no immediate winning move available to \(\mathrm{x}\) on this turn. This can be verified by enumerating every legal landing square and confirming that none produces four consecutive \(\mathrm{x}\) pieces in any row, column or diagonal.

The same exhaustive check shows that \(\mathrm{o}\) likewise possesses only a single forcing reply: column 5. Playing there completes the three-in-a-row \((3,4)\)-\((4,5)\)-\((5,6)\) whose open end \((2,3)\) is already a legal landing square. All other columns leave \(\mathrm{o}\) with at most two consecutive pieces and therefore create no immediate threat.

That forcing line, however, is not dangerous enough to compel a purely defensive answer from \(\mathrm{x}\). The square that would be occupied by the forced block, \((2,3)\), is itself an exceptionally strong building square for \(\mathrm{x}\):
- it raises the occupancy of five separate live winning lines (the horizontal window on row 2, the vertical window on rows 0-3 of column 3, and three distinct down-left diagonals);
- four of those lines become two-in-a-rows, each of which can be extended into a genuine threat on a subsequent turn;
- the same stone permanently kills \(\mathrm{o}\)'s only two-in-a-row that currently possesses two playable ends.

Because the forced reply is so favourable to the side that is forced, an optimal opponent will simply refuse to play the “force.” Consequently \(\mathrm{x}\) is free to claim the square voluntarily on the present move, simultaneously obtaining the best offensive development available and eliminating \(\mathrm{o}\)'s only tactical resource.

All other legal columns produce strictly inferior results:
- column 5 blocks the same tactical line but adds only a single (poisoned) two-in-a-row whose extension immediately hands \(\mathrm{o}\) a winning stone at \((2,5)\);
- column 4 creates only one new two-in-a-row;
- the remaining columns create none.

Thus column 3 is the unique move that maximises both immediate structural gain and long-term control of the live winning sets.

Final Answer: 3

